-0.42366498708724976,-6.270212179515511e-05,-0.00020668754586949944,-6.079655122448457e-06],"request":{"active_prefix":"","input_ids":[151644,8948,198,2610,525,1207,16948,11,3465,553,54364,14817,13,1446,525,264,10950,17847,13,151645,198,151644,872,198,50,3948,279,3491,3019,553,3019,13,20678,697,1590,4226,304,1124,79075,90,1112,92,382,10061,400,69,2075,15087,387,264,729,1741,429,369,678,400,87,4779,400,69,2075,8,284,282,2075,488,220,16,8,488,282,2075,481,220,16,568,3,220,16246,429,400,69,7,16,20,8,284,220,17,15,3,323,400,69,7,17,15,8,284,220,16,20,4779,12564,400,69,7,17,15,16,20,17,15,16,20,568,3,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nLet $f(x)$ be a function such that for all $x,$ $f(x) = f(x + 1) + f(x - 1).$  Given that $f(15) = 20$ and $f(20) = 15,$ compute $f(20152015).$","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"3a06947d31691cba40d873bfa71381aed6cb94523de23cfa4a624a1607c9fd49"},"seconds":56.31433153152466,"seed":650521158826606465,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve this problem, we need to analyze the given functional equation and use the provided values to find a pattern or a specific form of the function $f(x)$.\n\nGiven:\n- $f(x) = f(x + 1) + f(x - 1)$ for all $x$.\n- $f(15) = 20$ and $f(20) = 15$.\n\nLet's start by considering a few values around $x = 15$ and $x = 20$ to see if we can identify a pattern or specific values.\n\nFirst, we substitute $x = 16$:\n\\[f(16) = f(17) + f(15).\\]\nSince we do not have $f(17)$, let's consider $x = 19$:\n\\[f(19) = f(20) + f(18).\\]\nWe can continue this process, but let's first try to find $f(14)$ and $f(16)$ using the information we have.\n\nUsing $x = 15$:\n\\[f(15) = f(16) + f(14).\\]\nGiven $f(15) = 20$, we have:\n\\[20 = f(16) + f(14).\\]\n\nNow, consider $x = 14$:\n\\[f(14) = f(15) + f(13).\\]\nGiven $f(15) = 20$, we have:\n\\[f(14) = 20 + f(13).\\]\nThus, we have:\n\\[20 = f(16) + 20 + f(13) \\implies f(16) + f(13) = 0.\\]\n\nNext, consider $x = 19$:\n\\[f(19) = f(20) + f(18).\\]\nGiven $f(20) = 15$, we have:\n\\[f(19) = 15 + f(18).\\]\nNow, consider $x = 18$:\n\\[f(18) = f(19) + f(17).\\]\nThus, we have:\n\\[f(19) = 15 + f(18).\\]\n\\[f(18) = f(19) + f(17).\\]\n\nFrom the above equations, we can see that the values of $f(x)$ exhibit a pattern that repeats every 6 terms. To confirm this, we can use the functional equation to find a few more terms and check for periodicity.\n\nLet's denote:\n\\[f(15) = a = 20,\\]\n\\[f(16) = b.\\]\nThen we have:\n\\[f(14) = 20 - b.\\]\n\\[f(17) = -b.\\]\n\\[f(18) = -20.\\]\n\\[f(19) = 15.\\]\n\\[f(20) = c = 15.\\]\n\\[f(21) = -15.\\]\n\\[f(22) = -15.\\]\n\\[f(23) = 0.\\]\n\\[f(24) = 15.\\]\n\\[f(25) = 15.\\]\n\\[f(26) = 0.\\]\n\nFrom this, we can see that $f(x+6) = f(x)$. Therefore, the function $f(x)$ is periodic with period 6.\n\nTo find $f(20152015)$, we need to determine the remainder when 20152015 is divided by 6:\n\\[20152015 \\mod 6 = 5.\\]\nThus, $f(20152015) = f(5)$. From our periodic pattern, we know:\n\\[f(5) = f(19) = 15.\\]\n\nTherefore, the value of $f(20152015)$ is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{15}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{15}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
